WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.502
(host)
The year is 1924.

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:03.837
In a fog-draped gorge

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.880
near the foothills
of Mount St. Helens,

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:09.884
a group of gold prospectors
encounter a pack of ape men

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:11.845
near their remote cabin.

00:00:11.928 --> 00:00:13.471
Fearing for their lives,

00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:16.182
one of the group, Fred Beck,
fires his rifle at the beasts,

00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:19.728
wounding one and scaring
them off for the moment.

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:21.312
Later that night,

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:23.648
the prospectors wake
to their cabin under attack.

00:00:23.732 --> 00:00:25.316
Having returned under
cover of darkness,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.529
the beasts hurled stones and
pounded on the walls for hours,

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:31.948
finally retreating
after sunrise.

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:34.909
The terrified prospectors
fled back to their Oregon town

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:37.370
and told their tale to
all who would hear it,

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:38.621
earning the remote spot

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:41.416
the name Ape Canyon
after the incident.

00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:43.543
This was not the first
story of an encounter

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:46.087
with massive bipedal
hairy ape-like beasts

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:47.547
in North America.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.675
Other stories go back
centuries across the continent,

00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:53.720
but why does the legend
of this cryptid endure?

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:55.555
And what can Sasquatch teach us

00:00:55.638 --> 00:00:57.098
about the anxieties we may have

00:00:57.182 --> 00:01:00.477
about the wild world around
us and our place in it?

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.187
[triumphant music]

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.648
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:09.694
Sasquatch is often described
as a large bipedal primate

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:12.363
or dark hair-covered
humanoid varying in height

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:15.617
from 6 to 15 feet with
broad muscular shoulders,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.912
little or no neck, and
long ape-like arms.

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:20.538
Many sightings report
that the creature

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:22.665
appears more human than beast,

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.542
while others report glowing eyes

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:27.670
or a salivating
blood thirsty moth.

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.214
Our modern cultural
consciousness

00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:30.799
positions this
mysterious creature

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:32.550
solidly in the 20th century,

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.469
placed there by the mass media

00:01:34.552 --> 00:01:37.639
who often fail to report that
this monster origin stories

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:40.600
go back hundreds, if
not thousands of years,

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:43.645
and predate European
colonization of the new world.

00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:46.272
The Sts'ailes people are
a First Nations community

00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:49.734
located in the Fraser Valley
of British Columbia, Canada.

00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:51.611
They tell of a shape-shifting
forest protector

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:53.446
known as Sasq'ets.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.907
Sasquatch is an anglicized
version of Sasq'ets,

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.575
which translates
roughly to hairy man

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.702
in the Halkomelem language.

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.746
The Sts'ailes people
revere Sasq'ets

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.873
as a wild and elusive
guardian of the wilderness

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.875
who possesses
supernatural abilities

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.920
to protect and guide
those who respect

00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:11.089
and honor their
natural environment.

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:13.883
In their culture, Sasq'ets
is the very embodiment

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.678
of the benevolent
spirit of the forest.

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:18.972
A little over 1,000
miles to the south,

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:21.891
at the Tulle River Indian
Reservation in California,

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.060
ancient rock paintings
or pictographs

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:27.772
depict a small group of
Sasquatch known as The Family.

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.357
It is believed to be one

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.485
of the earliest recorded
appearances of the monster.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:34.237
The largest creature
in the pictograph

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.155
is thought to represent
a paternal figure

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:38.658
and is accompanied by
a mother and child.

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:40.326
Like Sasq'ets,
this hairy man figure

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.704
embodies traditional
human characteristics

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:45.540
like bipedal walking,
humanoid physiology,

00:02:45.623 --> 00:02:47.041
and community building,

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.420
while also evoking the more
elusive wildness of nature.

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:51.713
In some legends,

00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:54.215
they even had a hand
in humanity's creation.

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:55.592
But not all origin stories

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:56.968
from North American
indigenous folklore

00:02:57.051 --> 00:02:59.679
depict Sasquatch
as a benevolent guardian.

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:03.766
Some feature the being as a
darker lurking malevolent force.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.060
The Lummi people of
coastal Washington state

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:09.814
tell of massive bipedal
stalkers with furry white faces

00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:12.817
who carry misbehaving
children off into the night.

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:15.320
Several Iroquoian and
Algonquian language peoples

00:03:15.403 --> 00:03:18.448
share stories of
cannibalistic humanoid giants

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:22.327
known as stone giants,
stone men, or the Stoneclad.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:23.703
Depending on the story,

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:25.747
these forest dwellers cover
themselves with stones

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.459
or rub sand on their bodies
to callous their skin.

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:32.587
In other traditions, they were
born with impervious skin,

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.923
while still others say
their fur is so matted,

00:03:35.006 --> 00:03:37.091
it serves as pseudo-armor.

00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:38.551
All of these conditions

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:41.471
make stone giants
resistant to human weapons.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.765
Some of the most aggressive
versions of these monsters

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:46.017
have a particularly
grizzly penchant

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.937
for twisting the heads
off their human prey.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.357
And legends of bipedal
beasts persist for centuries,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.402
even after European colonialism
arrives in the Americas.

00:03:56.486 --> 00:03:59.530
Sixteenth century Spanish
explorers and Mexican settlers

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:02.617
told of creatures called
Los Vigilantes Oscuros

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.703
or dark watchers,
tall shadowy beings

00:04:05.787 --> 00:04:06.704
that appear on the peaks

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.123
of California's
Santa Lucia Mountains

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:12.794
during twilight, watching over
the land and its inhabitants.

00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:15.046
The lore of the watchers
even made its way

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:19.509
into a 1938 short story by
John Steinbeck called "Flight,"

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:21.511
which describes the
monsters as menacing

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.680
but relatively harmless figures

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.306
who wouldn't bother
a visitor of the forest

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.185
if they stayed on the trail
and minded their own business.

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:33.147
In 1847, Irish Canadian
adventurer and artist Paul Kane

00:04:33.231 --> 00:04:35.525
recounted stories told to
him by the Chinook people

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.652
about Skookums, wild men

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:39.654
who lived on the peaks
of Mount St. Helens

00:04:39.737 --> 00:04:41.698
and engaged in cannibalism.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:43.324
Kane offered cash rewards

00:04:43.408 --> 00:04:45.159
for anyone who could
take him on a journey

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:46.953
to find the fabled Skookums.

00:04:47.036 --> 00:04:48.579
But no such trek ever took place

00:04:48.663 --> 00:04:51.457
and the stories remained legend.

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:53.584
And perhaps the most
infamous account

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:56.754
came from none other than former
president Teddy Roosevelt.

00:04:56.838 --> 00:04:59.590
In his 1893 book,
"The Wilderness Hunter,"

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:01.551
Roosevelt details
a story told to him

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.595
by a grizzled old
mountain man named Bauman.

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:07.223
Bauman fearfully recounts
his personal experience

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:09.517
with a foul-smelling
bipedal creature

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.519
that ransacked
his trapping camp,

00:05:11.602 --> 00:05:13.688
left large prints, stalked him,

00:05:13.771 --> 00:05:17.233
and ultimately killed one of
his men by snapping his neck.

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:20.486
While neither Roosevelt nor
Bauman named the creature,

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.573
these descriptions have become
hallmarks of Sasquatch lore,

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.784
namely the foul smell
and oversized footprints.

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:28.786
This brings us to
the 20th century.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.622
After the Ape Canyon
incident of 1924,

00:05:31.706 --> 00:05:34.000
the next couple of decades
were relatively quiet

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:35.752
in terms of Sasquatch lore,

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:37.920
but advancements in media
starting in the mid 20th century

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.590
allowed for broader
dissemination

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.800
of mysterious monster sightings.

00:05:42.884 --> 00:05:46.137
The 1948 introduction of
the Polaroid instant camera

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.973
alongside the development of
colored broadcast television

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:50.767
and the growing
presence of TV sets

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.351
in the American household

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:56.105
made it easier to feed a growing
appetite for tabloid tales.

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:59.484
Like when in 1958, a Bluff
Creek California logger

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.486
named Jerry Crew
discovered a series

00:06:01.569 --> 00:06:03.863
of oversized
human-like footprints

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:06.365
near a bulldozer
on his job site.

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:08.534
The 16 inches long footprints

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:11.496
frightened and intrigued
Crew and his fellow loggers.

00:06:11.579 --> 00:06:12.830
When he returned to town,

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:15.458
he brought his story
to Andrew Genzoli,

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.503
a reporter for the
Humboldt Times newspaper.

00:06:18.586 --> 00:06:21.089
Genzoli interviewed the loggers
and published an article

00:06:21.172 --> 00:06:23.841
about the event complete
with a photo of Crew

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:26.552
holding a cast of one
of the footprints.

00:06:26.636 --> 00:06:27.804
It was in this article

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:31.015
that our monster earned
its new name, Bigfoot.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:33.101
When given an English
language-based name

00:06:33.184 --> 00:06:35.061
that was both simple
and evocative,

00:06:35.144 --> 00:06:38.272
the monster became
a national sensation.

00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:40.024
Under the new moniker,
older alleged sightings,

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:43.820
including the 1924
Ape Canyon incident,

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.823
became widely known and new
encounters were reported

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:48.866
via the emerging
mass media network

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.869
of American TV,
newspapers, and radio.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:54.205
Less than 10 years
later in 1967,

00:06:54.288 --> 00:06:57.208
another new technology,
home movie cameras,

00:06:57.291 --> 00:06:59.085
gave rise to the
most famous piece

00:06:59.168 --> 00:07:02.672
of documented Bigfoot
lore that exists to date,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.800
the one virtually everyone
has seen or seen spoofed,

00:07:05.883 --> 00:07:08.261
the Patterson-Gimlin film.

00:07:08.344 --> 00:07:10.972
Shot on October 20th, 1967,
in Bluff Creek, California

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:14.308
by two friends and
Bigfoot enthusiasts,

00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:16.602
the film clip captures
what appears to be

00:07:16.686 --> 00:07:19.939
a large bipedal creature
trudging along a wooded hillside

00:07:20.022 --> 00:07:21.858
in broad daylight
for almost a minute.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:25.027
To this day, it
remains the most iconic

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.487
and controversial
piece of evidence

00:07:26.571 --> 00:07:28.948
of the existence of Sasquatch.

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:30.867
It doesn't take a
film forensics expert

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:32.743
to see that the
Patterson-Gimlin film

00:07:32.827 --> 00:07:35.204
could easily be replicated
by any small group of people

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:38.749
with a camera and a bit
of costuming know-how.

00:07:38.833 --> 00:07:40.042
While there have
been confessions

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:42.587
and recently even AI evidence

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.214
that the footage does not
depict a mysterious monster,

00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:47.049
for many, it still
stands as evidence

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.343
of Bigfoot's existence.

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:50.720
Some other familiar sightings

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.139
have been much more
reliably debunked.

00:07:53.222 --> 00:07:56.726
In 1982, a retired logger
in his 80s, Rant Mullens,

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:58.394
admitted to having tormented

00:07:58.477 --> 00:08:01.397
a small group of gold
prospectors in Ape Canyon

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:02.773
when he was a young man.

00:08:02.857 --> 00:08:04.442
He said that he and his uncle

00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:06.068
had thrown stones at their
cabin from a rocky overlook

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:09.155
in a playful attempt
to scare them.

00:08:09.238 --> 00:08:11.741
Then later, having heard
that the prospector's tale

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:14.368
about fearsome ape men
made national news,

00:08:14.452 --> 00:08:15.620
Mullens claims to
have perpetrated

00:08:15.703 --> 00:08:17.997
several other Sasquatch hoaxes

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.416
in the region in the
years that followed.

00:08:20.499 --> 00:08:22.710
In 2002, the family
of Ray Wallace,

00:08:22.793 --> 00:08:24.712
a deceased coworker of
our footprint-finding

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:26.547
logger friend Jerry Crew

00:08:26.631 --> 00:08:28.049
revealed that they
had found a collection

00:08:28.132 --> 00:08:31.886
of large carved wooden feet
stored in his basement,

00:08:31.969 --> 00:08:33.804
which Wallace had
purportedly used

00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:37.058
to make the infamous footprints
in Bluff Creek back in 1958,

00:08:37.141 --> 00:08:40.436
meaning even the origins
of Bigfoot's name

00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:43.356
was the result of
a well-perpetrated hoax.

00:08:43.439 --> 00:08:46.776
And yet, despite all the
evidence against its existence,

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:48.736
Sasquatch remains firmly fixed

00:08:48.819 --> 00:08:50.863
in our cultural
psyche to this day.

00:08:50.947 --> 00:08:53.741
Why, and why does it seem
that the public perception

00:08:53.824 --> 00:08:55.993
of the monster has
shifted over time,

00:08:56.077 --> 00:08:59.163
from friendly to fearsome
and back to friendly again?

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:03.292
Many films portray Sasquatch
as a fearsome monster,

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:05.086
like "The Legend
of Boggy Creek,"

00:09:05.169 --> 00:09:06.462
the much maligned
"Cry Wilderness,"

00:09:06.545 --> 00:09:07.797
and the properly terrifying

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.841
2013 indie horror
"Willow Creek."

00:09:10.925 --> 00:09:12.760
And the image of
Sasquatch as a terror

00:09:12.843 --> 00:09:15.221
has inspired some
gripping novels as well,

00:09:15.304 --> 00:09:16.264
like "Devolution" by Max Brooks

00:09:16.347 --> 00:09:19.392
and "Roanoke Ridge"
by J.J. Dupuis.

00:09:19.475 --> 00:09:20.601
But the version of Sasquatch

00:09:20.685 --> 00:09:22.478
many are most
familiar with today

00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:24.814
is much more benign
and friendly.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:26.983
It's one that we see as
a mascot for sports teams

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:30.778
or as a spokesperson
for snack foods or beer

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:33.656
or a playful character
appearing in video games.

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:34.949
We might owe this return

00:09:35.032 --> 00:09:37.159
to a somewhat softer
image of Sasquatch

00:09:37.243 --> 00:09:40.121
to the 1987 film, "Harry
and the Hendersons,"

00:09:40.204 --> 00:09:41.664
which imagined the monster

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.041
as a benevolent and lovable
creature of the woods

00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:47.378
whose existence is threatened
by human encroachment.

00:09:47.461 --> 00:09:48.421
This iteration of Sasquatch

00:09:48.504 --> 00:09:49.964
coincides with growing
public concerns

00:09:50.047 --> 00:09:53.676
about environmental
issues in the 1980s.

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:54.927
The film's protagonist
is a reluctant hunter

00:09:55.011 --> 00:09:58.222
who falls in love
with a titular monster

00:09:58.306 --> 00:10:00.224
after hitting him with
the family station wagon

00:10:00.308 --> 00:10:01.809
on a weekend excursion.

00:10:01.892 --> 00:10:04.895
He and his family fight against
malevolent outside forces

00:10:04.979 --> 00:10:06.230
hunting the beast

00:10:06.314 --> 00:10:08.232
and are the only ones
who recognize Bigfoot

00:10:08.316 --> 00:10:10.526
as a precious and
vulnerable entity,

00:10:10.609 --> 00:10:12.028
much like our
natural environment

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:15.114
and wilderness spaces
who need saving.

00:10:15.197 --> 00:10:18.576
In a way, the lore of
Sasquatch has come full circle

00:10:18.659 --> 00:10:20.411
as our modern perceptions
of the monster

00:10:20.494 --> 00:10:21.996
are an embodiment of respect

00:10:22.079 --> 00:10:24.248
for our mysterious
natural surroundings.

00:10:24.332 --> 00:10:26.334
Much like the earliest
lore of Sasquatch

00:10:26.417 --> 00:10:28.711
from the indigenous
cultures of North America.

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:30.087
Debunked sightings, hoaxes,

00:10:30.171 --> 00:10:33.049
and an often cartoonish
representation in pop culture,

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:36.677
and yet the legend of Sasquatch
still has fervent believers.

00:10:36.761 --> 00:10:37.803
Why is that?

00:10:37.887 --> 00:10:39.472
Ecologist Robert Pyle

00:10:39.555 --> 00:10:41.557
contends that, "There seems
to be a general human need

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.685
"to create folklore about
larger than life creatures

00:10:44.769 --> 00:10:46.354
"with names usually translating

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:49.357
to something along the lines
of wild man or hairy man."

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.192
He writes that,
"These mythical creatures

00:10:51.275 --> 00:10:52.818
"represent a symbolic link

00:10:52.902 --> 00:10:55.613
"to the untamed wilderness
that surrounds us,

00:10:55.696 --> 00:10:56.697
"a connection to our world

00:10:56.781 --> 00:10:59.283
that modern society
has largely lost."

00:10:59.367 --> 00:11:00.659
It also wasn't so long ago

00:11:00.743 --> 00:11:03.579
that our ancestors were
evolved to walk upright.

00:11:03.662 --> 00:11:06.040
Using folklore,
particularly monsters,

00:11:06.123 --> 00:11:07.666
becomes a way to outsource

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.586
or displace our primal
fears of the unknown world,

00:11:10.669 --> 00:11:13.547
our unknown past, and
our unknown future.

00:11:13.631 --> 00:11:15.132
We are scared of these creatures

00:11:15.216 --> 00:11:17.676
that are not human but act human

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.470
and look more like
us and our ancestors

00:11:19.553 --> 00:11:21.472
than we may want to admit.

00:11:21.555 --> 00:11:22.765
Perhaps it is no coincidence

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:25.017
that the ancient lore
of Sasquatch evolved

00:11:25.101 --> 00:11:27.770
and endured right alongside
the last century and a half

00:11:27.853 --> 00:11:29.730
of North American
industrialization

00:11:29.814 --> 00:11:31.232
and modernization.

00:11:31.315 --> 00:11:33.692
From our fears of the
unknown American frontier

00:11:33.776 --> 00:11:35.277
to our modern anxieties

00:11:35.361 --> 00:11:37.822
about humanity's effects
on our fragile environment.

00:11:37.905 --> 00:11:39.949
Whether Sasquatch is
a malevolent monster

00:11:40.032 --> 00:11:42.993
or a misunderstood beast just
trying to protect his realm,

00:11:43.077 --> 00:11:44.995
one thing seems clear.

00:11:45.079 --> 00:11:48.499
The lore is likely to be
around for a long time to come.

00:11:48.582 --> 00:11:50.209
Reminding us to keep an eye

00:11:50.292 --> 00:11:52.420
on the big footprints
that we leave behind

00:11:52.503 --> 00:11:54.338
as we move through this world.

00:11:55.965 --> 00:11:58.175
Before you go,
I want to talk about death.

00:11:58.259 --> 00:12:01.011
No, not monstrous death,
but death-death.

00:12:01.095 --> 00:12:02.555
Or rather, I want to tell you

00:12:02.638 --> 00:12:06.183
about a new series on PBS
Voices called "Dead and Buried."

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:07.893
It's a show that explores
cultural perspectives

00:12:07.977 --> 00:12:09.979
and taboos surrounding death

00:12:10.062 --> 00:12:11.689
and invite you to reconsider

00:12:11.772 --> 00:12:13.274
how you think about
the inevitable.

00:12:13.357 --> 00:12:14.442
We've got a link down below.

00:12:14.525 --> 00:12:16.444
Tell them "Monstrum" sent ya.

00:12:16.527 --> 00:12:19.071
In the Halkomelem language.

00:12:19.155 --> 00:12:20.656
Halkomelem.

00:12:20.739 --> 00:12:22.783
Halkomelem.
Should we do that again?

00:12:22.867 --> 00:12:24.660
I think we should just do
that again for posterity.

00:12:24.743 --> 00:12:26.537
Halkomelem.

